+Ambiguity-aware revision:
+Prioritize the largest supported discrepancies across the entire image. Shared anchor
+motion can suggest a camera mismatch, but shared geometry assumptions can produce a
+similar pattern. A camera change can compensate for misplaced geometry. Neither a
+pattern nor a scalar improvement proves a unique cause. Retain competing explanations
+when evidence does not discriminate them; unresolved is a valid conclusion.
+Before revising, write a brief change rationale in program comments: observed discrepancy,
+alternative explanation, parameter family to change, expected projected movement, and
+well-matched anchors to preserve. Separate camera parameters from object placement,
+size and depth. Prefer the smallest coherent revision supported by multiple anchors.
+Avoid simultaneously changing unrelated camera, geometry and appearance parameters
+merely because they are adjustable. Coupled changes are appropriate only when supported
+by the reference and their roles are stated; do not present them as a controlled test.
+
+If controlled observations are supplied, use the actual recorded decision and measurements,
+not an earlier forecast or provisional diagnosis. Respect an unresolved decision. Distinguish
+forecast compatibility from causal attribution. Inspect global and fixed-reference-region
+results separately before considering their aggregate: a positive aggregate can conceal a
+global regression. Do not transfer a tested direction or magnitude automatically to a new
+image. If scores are unavailable, use a qualitative full-frame anchor comparison and do
+not invent numerical gains. Keep the comparison region fixed when interpreting supplied
+regional measurements; a moving region changes what is measured.
+An isolated-object view can clarify membership or silhouette, but removal of background
+changes appearance scores and does not justify deleting the surrounding scene.
+Scaling around an object's geometry center does not directly test transverse placement.
+Moving its center along its camera ray largely preserves center projection while changing
+apparent size; this does not test arbitrary height or support-plane displacement. Focal and
+pitch interventions have different whole-frame effects. A response to any one intervention
+may be compatible with more than one explanation.
+Prefer preserving a plausible complete build to an unsupported revision driven by one
+region. After a revision, audit both intended corrections and previously matched anchors.
+Report remaining uncertainty in comments; do not claim an unrendered change is verified.
+Keep the scene's editable groups, meaningful child parts and support relations intact.
+
+Validation boundaries:
+Keep visual fit, execution validity and emitted-part integrity separate. Passing structure,
+material or composite checks does not establish semantic completeness or true geometry.
+Missing support measurements are unknown, not proof of contact. AABB contact tests are
+approximations. Triangle crossings may be intentional joints; they are not semantic collision
+truth and do not justify blindly separating all intersecting parts. Use reference evidence,
+part function and available diagnostics together. Never optimize by deleting meaningful
+parts or flattening the hierarchy merely to improve a mechanical diagnostic.
 
